 {
  "file": "05-payment-held.renew",
  "source": "# A variable rate mortgage where the payment\n# stays the same when the rate moves. Many\n# Canadian variable mortgages work this way: a\n# rate rise means less of each payment goes to\n# the principal, so the loan takes longer to pay\n# off. If the rate rises far enough, the payment\n# only covers the interest. That rate is the\n# trigger rate. The rates are an example path,\n# not a forecast.\n\nmortgage \"Payment adjusts\"\n  balance $400,000\n  rate 4.2% then 5.5% after 6 months\n  amortization 25 years\n  term 3 years\n\nscenario \"Payment held\"\n  rate 4.2% then 5.5% after 6 months payment held\n\ncompare by payment, interest, balance, payoff, trigger rate\n    over 3 years\nshow trigger rate of \"Payment held\"\nexplain \"Payment held\"\n\n# At renewal the payment is worked out again\n# over the amortization that is left.\nscenario \"Fixed, then renews\"\n  rate 4.2% then 5.5% after 6 months payment held\n  renews at 5%\nshow renewal payment of \"Fixed, then renews\"\n\n# How far can the rate rise after 6 months before\n# the held payment stops covering the interest?\ncheck trigger rate of \"Payment held\" > 5.5%\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "06-trigger-boundary.renew",
  "source": "# The trigger rate is the rate at which a held\n# payment only covers the interest. For this\n# plan it starts at 6.53% (6.5301% to four\n# decimals; shown values are rounded down to a\n# thousandth, then trailing zeros are dropped).\n# After 6 months the\n# trigger on the balance left is 6.607%. A path\n# that stays just under that still runs: at\n# 6.60% the payment of $2,147.67 still covers\n# the interest, but only just, so the time to pay\n# off stretches to 106 years 1 month. That is how\n# long this payment would take if the rate stayed\n# there: a mathematical projection, not a contract\n# term. Mortgage contracts set their own rules near\n# the trigger rate. A path\n# that goes to 6.61% is refused: see\n# examples/refused/trigger-crossed.renew.\n# Renew does not model what a lender might do\n# instead, such as raising the payment to at\n# least the interest, converting the loan to a\n# fixed rate, asking for a lump sum, or letting\n# the balance grow up to a cap.\n\nmortgage \"Just under\"\n  balance $400,000\n  rate 4.2% then 6.60% after 6 months payment held\n  amortization 25 years\n  term 3 years\n\nshow trigger rate of \"Just under\"\nshow payment of \"Just under\"\nshow payoff of \"Just under\"\nexplain \"Just under\"\n"
 },
 {
  "file": "07-two-crossings.renew",
  "source": "# Break even when the answer changes twice.\n#\n# Everything paid over 5 years is not always\n# monotonic. For \"A\", a longer amortization means\n# a smaller payment, but a short one clears the\n# loan inside the 5 years and stops the payments.\n# So against \"B\", a 3 year loan, the answer to\n# \"does A pay less over 5 years?\" changes twice,\n# at 3 years and at 5 years 4 months, and Renew\n# lists both instead of picking one.\n#\n# The second pair crosses twice only 29 cents\n# apart: paid over 5 years rises with the yearly\n# lump until the loan is paid off inside the 5\n# years, then falls. The money search covers\n# every cent if the bound in docs/LANGUAGE.md\n# holds, so both changes are found.\n# The second change is a one cent difference:\n# at $15,997.97 \"Base\" pays $114,884.39 against\n# $114,884.40. It is real once every amount is\n# rounded to the cent, but it is a rounding\n# edge more than a money decision.\n#\n# Both lines use the short form of break even:\n# break even SETTING \"A\" vs \"B\". The long form,\n# break even SETTING for \"A\" against \"B\", is the\n# same statement.\n\nmortgage \"A\"\n  balance $400,000\n  rate 5%\n  amortization 25 years\n  term 1 year\n\nmortgage \"B\"\n  balance $400,000\n  rate 5%\n  amortization 3 years\n  term 3 years\n\nshow break even amortization \"A\" vs \"B\" by paid over 5 years\n\nmortgage \"Base\"\n  balance $100,000\n  rate 5%\n  amortization 25 years\n  term 5 years\n  lump $0 every year\n\nmortgage \"Ref\"\n  balance $329,215.72\n  rate 5%\n  amortization 25 years\n  term 5 years\n\nshow paid of \"Ref\"\nshow break even lump \"Base\" vs \"Ref\" by paid over 5 years\n"
 }
